<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we investigate extremal bounds on the Albertson index, a topological invariant that quantifies degree irregularity in graphs. The focus lies primarily on connected graphs and trees. We establish necessary and sufficient conditions for a connected graph <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve the maximum Albertson index, specifically that</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the minimum vertex degree. For trees <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with degree sequence <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\ldots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\ldots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and maximum degree <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we derive explicit upper and lower bounds on the minimum and maximum Albertson indices. These bounds utilize combinatorial and factorial expressions involving vertex degrees and tree parameters. Furthermore, the relationship between the Albertson index <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> of a tree and its first Zagreb index <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and degree sequence is given by</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)=M_{1}(T)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}+d_{n}-d_{1}-2n-2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)=M_{1}(T)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}+d_{n}-d_{1}-2n-2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">which offers a structural interpretation of irregularity in terms of vertex degrees. Additionally, we present inequalities linking the minimum and maximum Albertson indices through the maximum degree <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, refining the understanding of how degree sequences influence irregularity measures.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper. Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple, connected graph, where <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>. The minimum and the maximum degree of <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant\delta&lt;\Delta\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant\delta&lt;\Delta\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V_{1},V_{2},E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V_{1},V_{2},E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with two vertex sets <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}=|V_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}=|V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}d_{G}(v_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}d_{G}(v_{j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}d_{G}(v_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}d_{G}(v_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we say <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Optimally, <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite graph if its vertices can be partitioned into two sets such that every edge connects a vertex in <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> to a vertex in <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The maximum degree of a node had been defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> where the symmetric nodes are equal and we denote that by <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\max[d(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\max[d(V)]=|u|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\max[d(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\max[d(V)]=|u|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we say:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum[d(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\sum[d(V)]=|u|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum[d(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\sum[d(V)]=|u|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a path of order <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the star tree of order <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, typically denoted by <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In 1997, Albertson in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> mention to the imbalance of an edge <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="imb(uv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">imb(uv)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the irregularity (Albertson index) measure defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}\lvert d_{G}(u)-d_{G}(v)\rvert." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}\lvert d_{G}(u)-d_{G}(v)\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum degree of Albertson index and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> the minimum degree of Albertson index. Beyond generic graphs, special attention has been devoted to irregularity in trees with constrained degree sequences. Furthermore see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Molloy and Reed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and later Broutin and Marckert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> studied the asymptotic behavior of trees with fixed degree sequences. Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> characterized tree families via degree partitions and enumerated the number of realizable trees under such constraints. These results are instrumental in analyzing extremal indices among structured tree families.
Ali, A. et al in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> mention to total irregularity of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}(G)=(d(v_{1}),d(v_{2}),\dots,d(v_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}(G)=(d(v_{1}),d(v_{2}),\dots,d(v_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> a degree sequence of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v_{1})\geqslant d(v_{2})\geqslant\dots\geqslant d(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v_{1})\geqslant d(v_{2})\geqslant\dots\geqslant d(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=2(n+1)m-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}id(v_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=2(n+1)m-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}id(v_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">when <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}(G)=(k,k,\ldots,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}(G)=(k,k,\ldots,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is regular of degree <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, the graph is irregular. Ghalavand, A. et al. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> proved <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{T}(G)\leq n^{2}\operatorname{irr}(G)/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{T}(G)\leq n^{2}\operatorname{irr}(G)/4</annotation></semantics></math>, when the bound is sharp for infinitely many graphs. The first and the second Zagreb index, <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(G)=\sum_{v\in V(G)}d(v)^{2},\quad\text{and}\quad M_{2}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}d(u)d(v)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(G)=\sum_{v\in V(G)}d(v)^{2},\quad\text{and}\quad M_{2}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}d(u)d(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Sigma index had defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}(d_{G}(u)-d_{G}(v))^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}(d_{G}(u)-d_{G}(v))^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum degree of Sigma index and <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> the minimum degree of Sigma index. Bipartite graphs are additionally employed to analyse algebraic graphs, which have associative ring elements as vertices and edges. The study of algebraic graphs has applications in extreme graph theory and algebraic cryptography. Additionally, bipartite graphs are utilized in many fields of mathematics (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal of this paper, study of some bounds on trees among Albertson index by considering the special trees known caterpillar tree. Also, study of the extreme bounds of the two indices — Albertson index and Sigma index — as well as the study of these indices on bipartite graph theory, and discussion of these cases when the bipartite graph is complete and when it is incomplete.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we review the important concepts relevant to our work, including a literature survey of the most related papers. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we provide a preface covering some of the key theories and properties we have utilized to understand the work. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we introduce some bounds involving the extremal values of the Albertson and Sigma Indices. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we introduce some bounds involving the extremal values of the Albertson and Sigma Indices in bipartite graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through this paper, we point out that all the trees included in the study are caterpillar trees, we consider caterpillar trees denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of backbone (or path) vertices and <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of pendant vertices attached to each.
We denote by <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math> a caterpillar tree with a pair <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math> of vertices, where we refer to the <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> main vertices and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to pendent vertices. In fact, that is shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where we consider the main vertices given by sequence <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and the pendent vertices given with value <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math> for each vertices.</p>
