<div id="S2.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with minimum degree <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, maximum degree <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and vertex set of size <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)&gt;\frac{\delta(\Delta-\delta)^{2}\cdot n}{\Delta+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)&gt;\frac{\delta(\Delta-\delta)^{2}\cdot n}{\Delta+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next formula captures the Albertson index in caterpillar trees based on the degrees of the backbone vertices, and will be instrumental in deriving our closed-form expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Caterpillar tree with path vertices with degrees <math id="S2.Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},d_{2},\dots,d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},d_{2},\dots,d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=\left({{d_{n}}-1}\right)^{2}+\left({d_{1}-1}\right)^{2}+\sum\limits_{i=2}^{n-1}{\left({{d_{i}}-1}\right)\left({{d_{i}}-2}\right)}+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=\left({{d_{n}}-1}\right)^{2}+\left({d_{1}-1}\right)^{2}+\sum\limits_{i=2}^{n-1}{\left({{d_{i}}-1}\right)\left({{d_{i}}-2}\right)}+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdefinition2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-Maximum and Minimum)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a class of graphs, then maximum Sigma <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, minmum Sigma <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> of a graph <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, given by:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\sigma(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\sigma(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\sigma(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\sigma(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, the study of the extreme values (maximum and minimum) for each index requires certain conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Among the essential conditions commonly considered are the maximum degree <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimum degree <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Through Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we established the maximum value with the minimum degree <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with condition <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected graph of order <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has a maximum value of Albertson index if and only if <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has the maximum value of Albertson index among all connected graph of order <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> with the minimum degree <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\delta-1)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\delta-1)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\delta-1)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\delta-1)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, no two vertices of <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer where <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be an increasing degree sequence. Then, the upper bound of the minimum Albertson index is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\rceil}{(d_{n}-d_{1})!+(\Delta-1)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\rceil}{(d_{n}-d_{1})!+(\Delta-1)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequence where <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\leqslant\dots\leqslant d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\leqslant\dots\leqslant d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the condition <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}-d_{1}\geqslant\Delta-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}-d_{1}\geqslant\Delta-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{p}\leq 2^{n-\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{p}\leq 2^{n-\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\alpha\leq p\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\alpha\leq p\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the bound (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) provides a term for the minimum Albertson index satisfying</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\frac{2^{n-\Delta}}{(\Delta-1)!}\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\frac{2^{n-\Delta}}{(\Delta-1)!}\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the term <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, we note that <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil&lt;2^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil&lt;2^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the factorial terms satisfy <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{n}-d_{1})!\geqslant(d_{n-1}-d_{2})!\geqslant\dots\geqslant(d_{i+1}-d_{i})!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{n}-d_{1})!\geqslant(d_{n-1}-d_{2})!\geqslant\dots\geqslant(d_{i+1}-d_{i})!</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the inequality <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}\leq 2^{n+p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}\leq 2^{n+p}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at most <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, where</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}\leq n^{p}\leq 1+\Delta+\Delta^{2}+\dots+\Delta^{n}+\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}\leq n^{p}\leq 1+\Delta+\Delta^{2}+\dots+\Delta^{n}+\Delta(\Delta-1)^{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lower and upper bounds of the minimum Albertson index are then:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2^{n-\Delta}}{(\Delta-1)!}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\frac{2^{n-\Delta}}{(d_{i+1}-d_{i})!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2^{n-\Delta}}{(\Delta-1)!}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\frac{2^{n-\Delta}}{(d_{i+1}-d_{i})!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">From (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the term in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) satisfies</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}\left(2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil\right)=(n-\Delta)+\log_{2}\left(1+\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil\cdot 2^{-(n-\Delta)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}\left(2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil\right)=(n-\Delta)+\log_{2}\left(1+\lceil n\Delta^{2}/2\rceil\cdot 2^{-(n-\Delta)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Considering <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\alpha\leq p\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\alpha\leq p\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}&gt;\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}&gt;\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the bound</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq\frac{2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\rceil}{(d_{n}-d_{1})!+(\Delta-1)!}\leq\Delta-3" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq\frac{2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\rceil}{(d_{n}-d_{1})!+(\Delta-1)!}\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">holds. Hence, from (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the upper bound for the Albertson index can be compared with <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. This establishes that the term <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> grows the upper bound of the minimum Albertson index for <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;p&lt;n-\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;p&lt;n-\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, considering that <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\alpha}&lt;2^{p}\leq 2^{n-\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\alpha}&lt;2^{p}\leq 2^{n-\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geq\frac{2^{\alpha}}{\Delta-3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geq\frac{2^{\alpha}}{\Delta-3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">As desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be an increasing degree sequence, the maximum Albertson index <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, the minimum Albertson index <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The upper bound of the minimum Albertson index is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant\frac{(\Delta-2)^{3}}{n\Delta-\delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant\frac{(\Delta-2)^{3}}{n\Delta-\delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The lower bound of the minimum Albertson index is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leqslant\frac{\delta}{\Delta+1}n\Delta^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-\delta)}{6\delta(\Delta-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leqslant\frac{\delta}{\Delta+1}n\Delta^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-\delta)}{6\delta(\Delta-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
