<div id="S3.Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree, where <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V|</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|E|</annotation></semantics></math> with maximum degree <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and minmum degree <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, then a lower bound of Albertson index given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\log_{n}(\operatorname{irr}(T))\leqslant\frac{n(\Delta^{2}-\delta)+m}{m(\Delta-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\log_{n}(\operatorname{irr}(T))\leqslant\frac{n(\Delta^{2}-\delta)+m}{m(\Delta-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree. We know that <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T)&gt;n-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T)&gt;n-m</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leqslant n-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leqslant n-m</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to prove inequality (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). To do this, we first determine whether the bounds involving the Albertson index satisfy the lower bound (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">We observe that the upper bound for the Albertson index is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\frac{n(\Delta^{2}-\delta)}{m(\Delta-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\frac{n(\Delta^{2}-\delta)}{m(\Delta-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">This upper bound (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) remains valid when combining <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and considering that <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)\geq\Delta(\Delta-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)\geq\Delta(\Delta-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. By utilizing the properties of the natural logarithm, and considering the base as the number of vertices <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{n}(\operatorname{irr}(T))=\frac{\ln\operatorname{irr}(T)}{\ln n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{n}(\operatorname{irr}(T))=\frac{\ln\operatorname{irr}(T)}{\ln n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">From inequality (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), we then derive a lower bound:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\ln n\cdot\frac{n(\Delta^{2}-\delta)}{m(\Delta-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\ln n\cdot\frac{n(\Delta^{2}-\delta)}{m(\Delta-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, combining both the upper bound (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) and the lower bound (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), we obtain inequality (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) for <math id="S3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{n}(\operatorname{irr}(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{n}(\operatorname{irr}(T))</annotation></semantics></math> by considering <math id="S3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree with the maximum of Albertson index <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and the minmum Albertson index <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be an increasing degree sequence, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V(T)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V(T)|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|E(T)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|E(T)|</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}&lt;\Delta(T)(\operatorname{irr}_{\min}-n),\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}&lt;\Delta(T)(\operatorname{irr}_{\max}-n).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}&lt;\Delta(T)(\operatorname{irr}_{\min}-n),\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}&lt;\Delta(T)(\operatorname{irr}_{\max}-n).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order at least <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. The fact that <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;3(\operatorname{irr}(T)-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;3(\operatorname{irr}(T)-n)</annotation></semantics></math> is valid for <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;3(\operatorname{irr}(T)-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;3(\operatorname{irr}(T)-m)</annotation></semantics></math> is valid for <math id="S3.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S3.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an increasing degree sequence. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq\Delta(T)\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq\Delta(T)\left(\frac{n}{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.2.1" class="ltx_text">by restricting the maximum degree <math id="S3.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq n\Delta^{2}-m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq n\Delta^{2}-m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p21.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.3.1" class="ltx_text">The lower bound of the minimum Albertson index is</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\,\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\leq\Delta(T)\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\quad\text{and}\quad m\,\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\Delta(T)\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)\operatorname{irr}_{\max}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\,\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\leq\Delta(T)\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\quad\text{and}\quad m\,\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\Delta(T)\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)\operatorname{irr}_{\max}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">For an increasing degree sequence <math id="S3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, consecutive terms in a degree sequence from (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) use the constant term</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta+5)\frac{n}{2}+m-7." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta+5)\frac{n}{2}+m-7.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">This term scales the maximum vertex degree by a factor proportional to the graph size. We notice that:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+(\Delta-5)\frac{n}{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}|d_{i}+2|\,|d_{i}-1|+m-7." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+(\Delta-5)\frac{n}{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}|d_{i}+2|\,|d_{i}-1|+m-7.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the bound (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), and for the minimum Albertson index according to (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+\Delta(T)\frac{n}{5}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}|d_{i}+2|\,|d_{i}-1|+m+2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+\Delta(T)\frac{n}{5}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}|d_{i}+2|\,|d_{i}-1|+m+2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}-\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}-\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p24.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p24.2.1" class="ltx_text">then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p24.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p24.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\Delta\bigl(\operatorname{irr}_{\max}-\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\bigr)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\leq\Delta\bigl(\operatorname{irr}_{\min}-\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq\Delta\bigl(\operatorname{irr}_{\max}-\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\bigr)\quad\text{and}\quad\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\leq\Delta\bigl(\operatorname{irr}_{\min}-\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p24.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p24.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the bound (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is valid, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Albertson Index in Trees and Applications</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> considers a pair of trees, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with distinct degree sequences: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has a degree sequence dominated by vertices of degree 2, resembling a path graph, while <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is characterized by a degree sequence consisting of prime numbers. Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> focuses on a single tree with general degree properties, introducing a parameterized lower bound that depends on the maximum and minimum degrees, modulated by a scaling factor <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. In both lemmas, we provide bounds on the Albertson index.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a tow trees, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> an increasing degree sequence occur of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Tree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}=(b_{1},\dots,b_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}=(b_{1},\dots,b_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> an increasing degree sequence of prime number, consider <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V(T_{1})|,n_{2}=|V(T_{2})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V(T_{1})|,n_{2}=|V(T_{2})|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}=|E(T_{1})|,m_{2}=|E(T_{2})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}=|E(T_{1})|,m_{2}=|E(T_{2})|</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}=n_{1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}=n_{1}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{2}=m_{1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{2}=m_{1}-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T_{2})=\Delta(T_{1})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T_{2})=\Delta(T_{1})+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the bound of Albertson index given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq 5\cdot\frac{n_{1}\Delta_{2}^{3}+n_{2}\Delta_{1}^{4}+m_{1}\Delta_{2}^{2}}{\Delta_{1}(\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2})^{2}}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\max}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leq 5\cdot\frac{n_{1}\Delta_{2}^{3}+n_{2}\Delta_{1}^{4}+m_{1}\Delta_{2}^{2}}{\Delta_{1}(\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2})^{2}}\leq\operatorname{irr}_{\max}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V(T)|,m=|E(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V(T)|,m=|E(T)</annotation></semantics></math> with the maximum degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimum degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the lower bound of Albertson index is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{3n^{2}-10n}{2}\right\rfloor 2^{\alpha}+\Delta^{2}-n\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{3n^{2}-10n}{2}\right\rfloor 2^{\alpha}+\Delta^{2}-n\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that the Albertson index has many applications across trees, and one of the most important applications that we refer to in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is through a degree sequence according to a specific condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, a degree sequence is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}\geqslant\dots\geqslant d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩾</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}\geqslant\dots\geqslant d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> be the first Zagreb index of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, Albertson index of tree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(T)=M_{1}(T)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}+d_{n}-d_{1}-2n-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(T)=M_{1}(T)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}+d_{n}-d_{1}-2n-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> edges, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequence such that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}\geqslant\dots\geqslant d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩾</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}\geqslant\dots\geqslant d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\ell}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\ell}\in V</annotation></semantics></math> whose degree is at least three, to prove (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), we need to establish (<a href="#S3.E21" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We note that the first Zagreb index is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(T)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_{i}^{2}\leq m\left(\frac{2m}{n-1}+n-2\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(T)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_{i}^{2}\leq m\left(\frac{2m}{n-1}+n-2\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our goal is to relate this topological index, the first Zagreb index, to the degree sequence in a specific way. For the degree sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)=d_{1}^{2}+d_{n}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}+d_{n}-d_{1}-2n-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)=d_{1}^{2}+d_{n}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}+d_{n}-d_{1}-2n-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">From (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), it follows that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}^{2}+d_{n}^{2}\leq\frac{2m}{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}^{2}+d_{n}^{2}\leq\frac{2m}{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since every tree is a connected graph, we also have <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(T)\leq m(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(T)\leq m(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is known that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)=m(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)=m(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a star graph. Thus, from (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), we conclude</span></p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(T)=d_{1}^{2}+d_{n}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(T)=d_{1}^{2}+d_{n}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We completed discussing among Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> with degree sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math> given by etxtend to degree sequence in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> as <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik},d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik},d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> with consideration the term of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;d_{n-2}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{3}&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;d_{n-2}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{3}&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik},d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik},d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequences where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{k,\ell\}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{k,\ell\}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;d_{n-2}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{3}&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;d_{n-2}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{3}&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The caterpillar tree with such order has the maximum value of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>irr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}</annotation></semantics></math> among all caterpillar trees with such degrees sequence of path vertices.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any degree sequence of length <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1},</annotation></semantics></math>
such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=(d_{n}-1)^{2}+(d_{1}-1)^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=(d_{n}-1)^{2}+(d_{1}-1)^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, we need to prove that for <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, with the sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n},</annotation></semantics></math>
since <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\geq d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\geq d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can simplify the expression by modeling the association as <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{n}-1)^{2}=(d_{n}-1)(d_{n}-2)+(d_{n}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{n}-1)^{2}=(d_{n}-1)(d_{n}-2)+(d_{n}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, let <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=d_{n}+r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=d_{n}+r</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{n}-1)^{2}+(d_{1}-1)=d_{n}^{2}-d_{n}+r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{n}-1)^{2}+(d_{1}-1)=d_{n}^{2}-d_{n}+r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Also, for the sum <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}\left(d_{i}^{2}-3d_{i}+2\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}\left(d_{i}^{2}-3d_{i}+2\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then, <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)=d_{i}^{2}-3d_{i}+2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)=d_{i}^{2}-3d_{i}+2,</annotation></semantics></math>
where we consider <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}2=2(n-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}2=2(n-3)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can write:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)=\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}^{2}-3\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}+2(n-3)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}(d_{i}-1)(d_{i}-2)=\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}^{2}-3\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}+2(n-3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=(d_{n}-1)(d_{n}-2)+(d_{n}-1)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}^{2}-3\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}+2(n-3)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=(d_{n}-1)(d_{n}-2)+(d_{n}-1)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}^{2}-3\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}+2(n-3)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We suppose that (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) is maximal for <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The question is why we consider that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|-\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|-\sum_{i=2}^{n-1}d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">is maximal under this ordering, and we need to prove that this relationship remains maximal for <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">In this case, from (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) by comparing <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+(d_{1}+d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+(d_{1}+d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
with the main relationship</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{n}-1)(d_{n}-2)+(d_{n}-1)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}^{2}-3\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}+2(n-3)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{n}-1)(d_{n}-2)+(d_{n}-1)+\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}^{2}-3\sum_{i=2}^{n-2}d_{i}+2(n-3)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">we proceed further. Furthermore, consider the sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik},d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik},d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,\ell\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,\ell\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that if <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i1},d_{i2},\dots,d_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{j1},d_{j2},\dots,d_{j\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are even, then <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|k-\ell|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|k-\ell|=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and if they are odd, then <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|k-\ell|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|k-\ell|=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Here, the vertices of the sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math> occupy different positions, so the Albertson index in this case is defined as the sum of absolute differences over <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> terms:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\mathscr{D}}(T)=|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|+|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\mathscr{D}}(T)=|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|+|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The sum <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|</annotation></semantics></math> telescopes to <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{i1}-d_{ik}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{i1}-d_{ik}|</annotation></semantics></math> only if the sequence is strictly monotonic. However, since the values are absolute differences, direct telescoping is generally prohibited. Thus, we have the inequality <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{i1}-d_{ik}|\leq|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{i1}-d_{ik}|\leq|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, for the second sum, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|</annotation></semantics></math>
telescopes to <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{j1}-d_{j\ell}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{j1}-d_{j\ell}|</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{j1}-d_{j\ell}|\leq|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{j1}-d_{j\ell}|\leq|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|.</annotation></semantics></math> Therefore, according to these terms, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{i1}-d_{ik}|+|d_{j1}-d_{j\ell}|\leq|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|+|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{i1}-d_{ik}|+|d_{j1}-d_{j\ell}|\leq|d_{i1}-d_{i2}|+\dots+|d_{ik-1}-d_{ik}|+|d_{j1}-d_{j2}|+\dots+|d_{j\ell-1}-d_{j\ell}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">By considering <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i1}=d_{i,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i1}=d_{i,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i2}=d_{i,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i2}=d_{i,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the simplest and most general expression can be conveniently described using summation notation:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}_{\mathscr{D}}(T)=\sum_{m=1}^{k-1}|d_{i,m}-d_{i,m+1}|+\sum_{n=1}^{\ell-1}|d_{j,n}-d_{j,n+1}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}_{\mathscr{D}}(T)=\sum_{m=1}^{k-1}|d_{i,m}-d_{i,m+1}|+\sum_{n=1}^{\ell-1}|d_{j,n}-d_{j,n+1}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume that <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+d_{1}+d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+d_{1}+d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
is maximal when <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+d_{1}+d_{n+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+d_{1}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">is maximal when <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}&gt;d_{1}&gt;\dots&gt;d_{2}&gt;d_{n-1}.</annotation></semantics></math> We notice that <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}+d_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> attains its maximum value when <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, we need to prove that <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal under the same ordering. Thus,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}(G)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+d_{1}+d_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|+d_{1}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|d_{1}-d_{2}|+|d_{2}-d_{3}|+\dots+|d_{n-1}-d_{n}|+d_{1}+d_{n+1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|d_{1}-d_{2}|+|d_{2}-d_{3}|+\dots+|d_{n-1}-d_{n}|+d_{1}+d_{n+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, from (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) we consider two cases to determine the maximum value of <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 1:</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\geq d_{i+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\geq d_{i+1},</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=2d_{1}-d_{n}+d_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=2d_{1}-d_{n}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal. Note that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> arises from combining the two <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> terms, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}+d_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 2:</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\leq d_{i+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\leq d_{i+1},</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=d_{n}+d_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=d_{n}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal, so in this case, the term <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|</annotation></semantics></math> is combined with the maximum value <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}+d_{n+1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}+d_{n+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}2d_{1}-d_{n}+d_{n+1},&amp;\text{if }d_{i}\geq d_{i+1},\\
d_{n}+d_{n+1},&amp;\text{if }d_{i}\leq d_{i+1},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}2d_{1}-d_{n}+d_{n+1},&amp;\text{if }d_{i}\geq d_{i+1},\\
d_{n}+d_{n+1},&amp;\text{if }d_{i}\leq d_{i+1},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is maximal when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n+1}\geq d_{1}\geq\dots\geq d_{2}\geq d_{n},</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}+d_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}+d_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Hypothesize <a href="#Thmhypothesize1" title="Hypothesize 1. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we provide an application of Albertson index on Fibonacci numbers satisfy caterpillar tree.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmhypothesize1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_hypothesize">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmhypothesize1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hypothesize 1</span></span><span id="Thmhypothesize1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmhypothesize1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Fibonacci number with <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(F_{3},\dots,F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(F_{3},\dots,F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequence of Fibonacci number. Then, Albertson index of <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> among <math id="Thmhypothesize1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)=\sum_{i=3}^{n-1}F_{i}+\sum_{i=5}^{n-1}(F_{i}-2)\lvert F_{i}-1\rvert+\lvert F_{4}-1\rvert+(F_{n}-1)\lvert F_{n}-1\rvert+2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)=\sum_{i=3}^{n-1}F_{i}+\sum_{i=5}^{n-1}(F_{i}-2)\lvert F_{i}-1\rvert+\lvert F_{4}-1\rvert+(F_{n}-1)\lvert F_{n}-1\rvert+2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(F_{3},\dots,F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(F_{3},\dots,F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequence of Fibonacci number, then we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\lvert F_{3}-F_{4}\rvert+\dots+\lvert F_{n-1}-F_{n}\rvert=\sum_{i=3}^{n-1}F_{i}+2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\lvert F_{3}-F_{4}\rvert+\dots+\lvert F_{n-1}-F_{n}\rvert=\sum_{i=3}^{n-1}F_{i}+2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">According to Albertson index for every vertex, by considering the constant term is <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert F_{4}-1\rvert+(F_{n}-1)\lvert F_{n}-1\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert F_{4}-1\rvert+(F_{n}-1)\lvert F_{n}-1\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, we noticed that:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F_{5}-2)\lvert F_{5}-1\rvert+(F_{6}-2)\lvert F_{6}-1\rvert+\dots+(F_{n-1}-2)\lvert F_{n-1}-1\rvert=\sum_{i=5}^{n-1}(F_{i}-2)\lvert F_{i}-1\rvert." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F_{5}-2)\lvert F_{5}-1\rvert+(F_{6}-2)\lvert F_{6}-1\rvert+\dots+(F_{n-1}-2)\lvert F_{n-1}-1\rvert=\sum_{i=5}^{n-1}(F_{i}-2)\lvert F_{i}-1\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now, by applying (<a href="#S3.E24" title="In Proof. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>),(<a href="#S3.E25" title="In Proof. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) to Albertson index, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
