<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmexample1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(F_{3},\dots,F_{10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(F_{3},\dots,F_{10})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequence of Fibonacci number, where <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}=1,F_{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}=1,F_{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}=3</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}=F_{n-1}+F_{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}=F_{n-1}+F_{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Example 1. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we observed that, then Albertson index according to (<a href="#S3.E24" title="In Proof. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~F_{3}+\dots+F_{11}=1\sum_{i=3}^{10}F_{i}+2=143" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>143</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~F_{3}+\dots+F_{11}=1\sum_{i=3}^{10}F_{i}+2=143</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, from (<a href="#S3.E26" title="In Proof. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>),(<a href="#S3.E27" title="In Example 1. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) we present Albertson index as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)=143+4430+7746=12319." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>143</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4430</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7746</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12319</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)=143+4430+7746=12319.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
