<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bipartite graph with vertex sets <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V_{1}(\mathscr{G})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V_{1}(\mathscr{G})|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}=|V_{2}(\mathscr{G})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}=|V_{2}(\mathscr{G})|</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the condition <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}\leq n_{1}^{2}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}\leq n_{1}^{2}-2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(n_{1}^{2}-2)=\delta(n_{2}^{2}-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(n_{1}^{2}-2)=\delta(n_{2}^{2}-2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the following holds:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V_{1}(\mathscr{G})}d(v)+\sum_{u\in V_{2}(\mathscr{G})}d(u)=\delta(n_{1}^{2}-2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V_{1}(\mathscr{G})}d(v)+\sum_{u\in V_{2}(\mathscr{G})}d(u)=\delta(n_{1}^{2}-2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">From (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>), the vertex sets <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor\frac{n_{1}n_{2}}{3}\right\rfloor\geqslant n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)-\delta(n_{1}-2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor\frac{n_{1}n_{2}}{3}\right\rfloor\geqslant n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)-\delta(n_{1}-2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete bipartite graph, the Sigma index is given by <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)-\delta(n_{1}-2)+\left\lfloor\frac{n_{1}n_{2}}{3}\right\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)-\delta(n_{1}-2)+\left\lfloor\frac{n_{1}n_{2}}{3}\right\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Assuming <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}&lt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}&lt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&lt;\max\{n_{1},n_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&lt;\max\{n_{1},n_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) holds for the term <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)+\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{1}(n_{2}-\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)+\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{1}(n_{2}-\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">Considering the term <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}n_{2}+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}n_{2}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we establish the bound</span></p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta+\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}+\left\lfloor\frac{n_{1}n_{2}}{3}\right\rfloor." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta+\Delta(\delta-1)^{2}+\left\lfloor\frac{n_{1}n_{2}}{3}\right\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">From (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>), we observe that the term <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}\leqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta\quad\text{and}\quad n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}\leqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}\leqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta\quad\text{and}\quad n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}\leqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.7.1" class="ltx_text">by implying that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}\geqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}\geqslant n_{1}n_{2}+\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.8.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}}{n_{1}n_{2}+\delta}\leqslant\Delta(n_{1}-\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n_{1}\Delta+n_{2}(\delta-1)^{2}+n_{2}\Delta+n_{1}(\delta-1)^{3}}{n_{1}n_{2}+\delta}\leqslant\Delta(n_{1}-\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.9.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an incomplete bipartite graph, we establish the bound</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+\delta(\delta-1)^{3}+\frac{\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+\delta(\delta-1)^{3}+\frac{\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.10.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the relations (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E31" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) remain satisfied for the upper bound of the Sigma index, where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{2}\leqslant\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\delta\Delta^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{2}\leqslant\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\delta\Delta^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.11.1" class="ltx_text">and it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2n_{1}\delta^{2}}\leqslant\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\sqrt{2n_{1}n_{2}\delta\Delta^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2n_{1}\delta^{2}}\leqslant\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\sqrt{2n_{1}n_{2}\delta\Delta^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.12.1" class="ltx_text">Taking into account (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>), the required relation (<a href="#S4.E28" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) is satisfied in both the complete and incomplete bipartite graph.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> provide us an important result of the bound with <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph, with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V_{1}(\mathscr{G})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V_{1}(\mathscr{G})|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}=|V_{2}(\mathscr{G})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}=|V_{2}(\mathscr{G})|</annotation></semantics></math>. The maximum value of the Sigma index is bounded by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}^{4}+n_{2}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}^{4}+n_{2}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has a sharp bound.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n^{2})=n_{1}^{4}+n_{2}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n^{2})=n_{1}^{4}+n_{2}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n)=n_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n)=n_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then, by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> we presented the upper bound and the lower bound of <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has a sharp bound <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math>, then when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the upper bound of the Sigma index satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Similarly, the lower bound of the Sigma index satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n^{2})=n_{1}^{4}+n_{2}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n^{2})=n_{1}^{4}+n_{2}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n)=n_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n)=n_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the Albertson index on <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma(\mathscr{G})}\leq\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq\sqrt{(n_{1}+n_{2})\sigma(\mathscr{G})+n_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma(\mathscr{G})}\leq\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq\sqrt{(n_{1}+n_{2})\sigma(\mathscr{G})+n_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">According to Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we find that the maximum value of the Sigma index is bounded by <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, and this holds for the Sigma index bounded by <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 1:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is not the upper bound of the Sigma index. Then, we notice that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})}\leq\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq\sqrt{(n_{1}+n_{2})\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})}\leq\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq\sqrt{(n_{1}+n_{2})\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n^{2}),\quad\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n^{2}),\quad\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, we note that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the Sigma index satisfies the bound <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)+n_{1}(n_{2}-\delta)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}(n_{1}-\delta)+n_{1}(n_{2}-\delta).</annotation></semantics></math>
Then,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})-\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{1}^{4}\quad\text{and}\quad\sigma(\mathscr{G})-\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})-\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{1}^{4}\quad\text{and}\quad\sigma(\mathscr{G})-\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})&gt;n_{2}^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Thus, it should be <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})-\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\theta(n^{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})-\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\theta(n^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math>
That means <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper bound of the Sigma index.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 2:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, we need to prove that (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) is the lower bound of the Sigma index given the term.
If <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete bipartite graph, it satisfies <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}^{2}n_{2}-n_{2}^{2}n_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}^{2}n_{2}-n_{2}^{2}n_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then, according to (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), we find that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n^{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus, by considering <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2},</annotation></semantics></math>
we have the bound (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>), which presents the relationship with the Albertson index as</p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}+n_{2})\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}+n_{2})\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\sigma_{\max}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now, according to Case 1, we find that (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) is the upper bound of the Sigma index satisfying <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n).</annotation></semantics></math>
Then, when <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\geqslant\theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}+n_{2})\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}+n_{2})\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, we have</p>
<table id="S4.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}+n_{2})\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}+n_{2})\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Actually, both (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E36" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) remain correct when <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an incomplete bipartite graph. In this case, the bound holds as</p>
<table id="S4.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}|n_{1}-n_{2}|+n_{2}|n_{1}-n_{2}|)\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant(n_{1}|n_{1}-n_{2}|+n_{2}|n_{1}-n_{2}|)\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+\theta(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E36" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>), and (<a href="#S4.E37" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>), it follows that (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 On Bipartite Graph with Topological Indices ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
</section>
