<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through this paper, the study of the extreme values (maximum and minimum) for each index requires certain conditions. According to Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, the upper bound (the lower bound) of the minimum Albertson index is:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant\frac{(\Delta-2)^{3}}{n\Delta-\delta},\quad\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leqslant\frac{\delta}{\Delta+1}n\Delta^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-\delta)}{6\delta(\Delta-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant\frac{(\Delta-2)^{3}}{n\Delta-\delta},\quad\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\leqslant\frac{\delta}{\Delta+1}n\Delta^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-\delta)}{6\delta(\Delta-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Through Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we established the maximum value with the minimum degree <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with condition <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\delta-1)(2\delta-1)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1 Albertson Index in Trees and Applications ‣ 3 Bounds on Albertson Index with Given Degree Sequence ‣ Extremal Irregularity Bounds for Albertson Index in Trees and Bipartite Graphs with Degree Sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> focuses on a single tree with general degree properties, introducing a parameterized lower bound that depends on the maximum and minimum degrees, modulated by a scaling factor <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where the lower bound of Albertson index is</p>
<table id="S5.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{3n^{2}-10n}{2}\right\rfloor 2^{\alpha}+\Delta^{2}-n\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{3n^{2}-10n}{2}\right\rfloor 2^{\alpha}+\Delta^{2}-n\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The investigation of topological indices across arboreal structures, through the discernment of aberrant values, constitutes the theoretical underpinning for myriad pragmatic applications, a salient exemplar of which we have delineated in the present study.</p>
</div>
</section>
